page("seasoning-a-portable-grill-before-the-first-trip","Seasoning a Portable Grill Before the First Trip","How to season a raw carbon steel portable grill at home before your first outing.","balm",f"""
<p>Raw carbon steel needs seasoning before you cook on it. Do it at home a few days before the first trip.</p>
{ol(["Wash off shop dust, dry completely.","Wipe a very thin coat of food-safe oil or <a href='"+BALM+"'>Slot Grill Seasoning Balm</a> on the grates, griddle plates and inside surfaces. Wipe off the excess.","Assemble and run a moderate charcoal fire to heat the steel about an hour. (Slot Grill's balm instructions say 400°F for 1 hour.)","Cool and repeat 2 to 3 times."])}
<p>Slot Grill's FAQ says the makers like high-temperature BBQ paint on the grill body and keep grilling surfaces lightly oiled; the balm page calls balm a food-safe alternative to paint for the body. Never paint cooking surfaces.</p>
""","howto",[])

page("lighting-charcoal-away-from-home","Lighting Charcoal Away From Home","Reliable ways to light charcoal at a tailgate, campsite or beach without lighter fluid.","c28",f"""
{table(["Method","Pack","Notes"],[["Chimney starter","Chimney + paper","Fastest, most reliable; 15–20 minutes"],["Fire-starter cubes","Cubes","Light inside a small charcoal pile in the grill"],["Kindling (where allowed)","Nothing extra","Both Slot Grill models burn cooking wood too"]])}
<h2>Tips</h2>{ul(["Keep charcoal dry in a sealed bin.","Shield the chimney from wind while it catches.","Never use gasoline; never add lighter fluid to lit coals."])}
""","howto",[])

page("cooler-and-food-safety-for-portable-grilling","Cooler and Food Safety for Portable Grilling","Keeping food safe when you grill away from a kitchen: cooler packing, raw vs cooked, time limits and internal temperatures.","c08",f"""
{ul(["Keep the cooler at 40°F or below with plenty of ice; keep it shaded and closed.","Raw meat sealed at the bottom, below ready-to-eat food.","Separate tongs and trays for raw and cooked.","Don't leave perishables out more than 2 hours, or 1 hour above 90°F.","Wash hands, or use soap and water you brought."])}
"""+temps(),"howto",[])

page("controlling-heat-on-a-portable-grill","Controlling Heat on a Portable Grill","How to control heat on a lidless portable charcoal grill using coal amount, placement and two zones.","c08",f"""
<p>No knobs, no lid: you control heat with how many coals and where they sit.</p>
{table(["Want","Do"],[["Hotter","More coals, piled closer"],["Cooler","Fewer coals, spread thin"],["Both","Two-zone: coals on two-thirds, last third empty"],["Longer cook","Add a few lit coals every 30–45 minutes"]])}
<p>On the Slot Grill's two-piece top you can lift one half to add coals while food sits on the other.</p>
""","howto",[])

page("setting-up-on-different-surfaces","Setting Up a Portable Grill on Different Surfaces","Which surfaces are safe for a portable charcoal grill: sand, gravel, dirt, concrete, pavers, and why not decks, tables or truck beds.","full10",f"""
{table(["Surface","OK?","Notes"],[["Bare dirt, gravel","Yes","Level it; clear dry grass"],["Sand","Yes","Wiggle it level; never bury coals"],["Concrete, pavers","Yes","May discolor; a fire-safe pad helps"],["Fire-safe pad","Yes","Good for patios"],["Wood/composite deck, plastic, turf, grass","No","Combustible"],["Tailgate, truck bed, boat deck","No","Never cook on a vehicle"]])}
<p>Slot Grill's air gap (2⅞&quot; Compact, 3⅝&quot; Full Size) helps minimize radiant heat to the surface below, but the surface still must be non-combustible.</p>
""","howto",[])

page("handling-wind-when-grilling-outdoors","Handling Wind When Grilling Outdoors","How wind affects an open portable charcoal grill and how to cook safely on breezy days.","both02",f"""
{ul(["Wind feeds coals; they burn hotter and faster. Bring extra fuel.","Put the coal end into the wind; cook on the leeward side.","Shelter behind a vehicle or wall with safe clearance.","Watch for sparks; skip it on high-wind or red-flag days."])}
""","howto",[])

page("ash-disposal-and-leave-no-trace","Ash Disposal and Leave No Trace","How to put out and dispose of charcoal ash at parks, beaches, tailgates and campsites.","c13",f"""
{ol(["Let coals burn down.","Drown with water and stir, where allowed; repeat until cold to the touch.","Otherwise wait until fully cold.","Into a lidded metal can, or the site's hot-coal bin.","Pack out everything else."])}
<p>Never dump hot ash on grass, in a trash can, or bury it. Never pack a warm grill into a vehicle.</p>
""","howto",[])
